um I first saw Charles Burnett's  landmark film killer of sheep in high  school when my brother was a filmmaker  as well was putting together a  distribution collective for black  independent films back though back then  you couldn't see those film in theaters  one of those films in that collective  was killer of sheep even at the time I  was aware of that it was a privilege to  get a chance to see this movie and it  blew my mind on multiple levels the  black and white cinematography was  gorgeous the competition the  compositions were stunningly beautiful  and the music choices were impeccable  and to a small kid growing up in a whole  black town in the Midwest the characters  were utterly relatable the weight of the  film show Beauty and the midst of  despair remind me of the great  theologian smoked song ugly Beauty but  the work of art that I think bears the  closest resemblance to the film as a  whole is actually the great novel by  Ralph Ellison Invisible Man Ralph  Ellison does not have a large body of  work but no one questions the fact that  Invisible Man is one of the greatest  books of the 20th century and in that  same way that Invisible Man is a great  piece of American literature killer of  sheep is a great piece of American  cinema  killer of sheep does a very rare thing  it portrays a particular subculture with  great clarity accuracy and cultural  specificity and in doing so Craig's a  drama that is universally relatable  I've known Charles for many years he's a  quiet gentleman with a huge heart and it  is an incredible pleasure to get to see  him presented with an Oscar tonight on  behalf of the Academy Board of Governors  congratulations  [Applause]
 

